  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) Amidst the COVID pandemic, the Fiji Trade Union Congress (FTUC) carried out an analysis through a Rapid Assessment (RA) on the impact of COVID-19 on employment and their membership. Based on the findings of 3,000 FTUC member&apos;s from the most affected sectors recommendations were proposed through representation and submissions of proposals to the Government of Fiji (Ministry of Economy) to include the said proposals in the for the 2020/21 national budget/COVID-19 stimulus package designs, where nearly one third of the proposed solutions have been accepted by the government. 

2) Initially, FTUC was able to coordinate and assess training needs of 1,420 redundant workers in Fiji, of which 54% of the workers sought direct assistance of some form from the FTUC. In extending new services and building upon the need to reach out to new memberships, the FTUC signed a bipartite MOU with the Fiji Employers Federation to support redundant workers who have started micro enterprises in the informal sector, through a bipartite initiative called &quot;Transition to Business (T2B)&quot;.

3) The above initiative, led to proof of concept to influence policy makers, especially in the UN, to support a larger project (U$2.5m) targeting inform sector workers and extending services. The renewed interest, led to a Multi Partner Trust Fund (MPTF) Project to be launched in Fiji in which FTUC is a lead partner. As a result of the activities carried out by the MPTF Project, FTUC continues to reach out to the informal sector workers and through the new services of business development and rights coordination and greater digital usage.  

4) The FTUC (4 members, including youths) was supported to engage in national level discussions on SDG and contribute to the national and CSO report on the Voluntary National ILO Suva through initially from the RBSA Project and later through the MPTF Project and ACTRAV support and technical advice continues to support the FTUC in reaching out and expanding their service delivery to the informal sector workers.  The technical advice and interventions include the design of the initial concept on the employments services, the methodology and outreach strategies to the informal sector, planning and integrating SDG and Youth concerns in working out of informality, addressing and strengthen freedom of association and ensuring greater expansion of trade unionism, and potential digital tools development for outreach to informal economy workers.</narrative>
   </description>
